  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1: Another round of more potent thunderstorms this \nafternoon into evening. Storms have the potential to be \nstrong/severe.\n\nSevere Thunderstorm Watch in effect until 8 PM this evening area-\nwide.\n\nFirst impulse and associated rain has largely exited the area. Much \nof the previous activity was stratiform while more cellular \ndevelopment occurred near the western CWA. Some segments of strong \nstorms have quickly triggered across WV and will enter our NW \nshortly. This was supported earlier this morning with decent CAM \nconsensus and some scattering of the cloud deck has occurred \nallowing for destabilization. The upper NW flow looks to still \nsupport some shortwave impulse influencing dynamic forcing for \ncontinued development. Shear parameters look quite favorable for \norganized storm modes along with possible supercells interacting \nwith low level helicity. Still thinking damaging winds are the \nprimary threat then large hail and isolated tornadoes as secondary \nthreats. \n\nAs far as the flash flooding threat, there was already some training \ncells in the west earlier that prompted and verified warning \nissuance. This could continue to be an issue for other areas that \nsee similar cell trends with the transition into the afternoon round \nof convection. The morning sounding indicated about a 1.5 inch PWAT \nso these storms should be efficient precip producers.\n\nBy later this evening activity should wane and exit southward, then \nmuch of the rest of this week should be dry and dominated with an \nupper ridge of high pressure. The next main chance for precip looks \nto hold off until next weekend.",
